<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>1.8. Review of Vector Calculus</title>
  <p><alert>MTH 401</alert></p>

  <p>
    Review of section 1: Vector Calculus.  You are free to skip to the problems you struggled with most.
  </p>
  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>We computed each of the following in an earlier activity so I’d like you to remember
what you found when we did that:
<md>\nabla \times \nabla f, \quad\quad \nabla \cdot (\nabla \times {\bf F}), \quad \quad \nabla \cdot (\nabla f)</md></p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
       <md><mrow>\nabla \times \nabla f \amp = \vec 0,</mrow>
        <mrow>  \nabla \cdot (\nabla \times {\bf F})\amp = 0,  </mrow> 
        <mrow> \nabla \cdot (\nabla f)\amp =\nabla^2 f = \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2}+\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial y^2}+\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial z^2}</mrow></md></p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that <m>{\bf F}(x,y,z) = (-y\cos z, xe^z, xy)</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="3cm">
      <statement>
        <p>Find <m>\nabla \times {\bf F}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        <md>
          =(x-e^zx, -y+y\sin z, e^z+\cos z)
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="3cm">
      <statement>
        <p>Is <term>F</term> a conservative field?  Explain your response.</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        Nope.  If it was, then <m>\mbf F = \nabla f</m> for some <m>f</m>, 
        and 
      <m>\nabla \times (\nabla f)=0</m>.  Since the curl is not zero, it's not 
      conservative.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>S</m> is the upper hemisphere of radius 1 centered at the origin, and <m>{\hat \nvec}</m> is the normal direction to the curve with the vertical component pointing upward, find the flux of <m>{\bf F}</m> through <m>S</m> in the direction of <m>{\hat \nvec}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        Add in the bottom disk to make it a closed surface.  Then the whole 
        closed surface would be 
        <md>
          \oiint_S \mbf F \cdot \hat \nvec ~dA = \iiint_E \nabla \cdot \mbf F~dV = \iiint_E 0~dV = 0.
        </md>
        Thank you divergence theorem.  Through the bottom part you added, the normal
        vector is <m>\mbf n = (0,0,-1)</m>, so the flux through that piece would be
        <md>
          \iint_{disk} \mbf F \cdot \hat \nvec~dA = \iint_{disk} -xy~dA = 
          \int_{\theta=0}^{2\pi} \int_{r=0}^1 -r\cos\theta r \sin\theta r~drd\theta = 0.
        </md>
        (Would need to parameterize with polar coordinates here, <m>x=r\cos\theta, y=r\sin\theta</m> with
        <m>0\le r \le 1, 0\le \theta\le 2\pi</m>, and can use an integral solver to do that integral
        unless you conveniently remember the double angle formula (not required.)).
      </p>
      <p>
        So the flux through the top is the flux through the whole thing minus the flux 
        through the bottom disk.  All of which are 0.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that <m>{\bf F} (x,y,z) = (x^3yz, xy^3z,xyz^3)</m> and that <m>S</m> is the sphere of radius 3 centered at the origin.  Find the flux of <m>\nabla \times {\bf F}</m> through <m>S</m> in the outward direction, that is find <md>\oiint_S (\nabla \times {\bf F}) \cdot {\hat \nvec} ~dA</md></p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        Flux through a closed surface, seems divergence-theorem-esk.  And indeed,
        <md>
          \oiint_S (\nabla\times\mbf F)\cdot \hat \nvec~dA = \iiint_E \nabla \cdot (\nabla \times \mbf F)~dV = 0
        </md>
        see the first problem - the divergence of the curl.  Well that seems easier than not using the divergence theorem...
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="0.0in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that <m>{\bf F}(x,y) = (x,1)</m> and <m>C</m> is the 
        curve shown with normal <m>{\hat \nvec}</m>.  Find the flux of <m>{\bf F}</m> through <m>C</m> in the direction <m>{\hat \nvec}</m>.
      </p>
      <image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
      <shortdescription>The curve drawn in the plane, see description.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-9-pic1">
        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="0">
    
          <coordinates bbox="(-2,-1,2,3)">
            <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
            <line p1="(-1,0)" p2="(0,2)" stroke="blue" thickness="5"/>
            <line p1="(1,0)" p2="(0,2)" stroke="blue" thickness="5"/>
            <vector tail="(0.5,1)" v="(2/sqrt(5),1/sqrt(5))" stroke="red" thickness="2" />
            <label anchor="(1,1)" >
              <m>\hat \nvec</m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
      <description> 
        <p> The curve consists of two line segments, from the point <m>(-1,0)</m> to the point <m>(0,2)</m> and then from <m>(0,2)</m> to <m>(1,0)</m>. 
        Additionally, the normal direction <m>\hat \nvec</m> is shown by a vector with the tail at point <m>(0.5,1)</m> on the curve and traveling
        one unit upward and two units to the right.
        </p>
      </description>
    </image>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        Could find the <m>\hat \nvec</m> for the two pieces, and the flux through 
        the two pieces.  
      </p><p>Or could close the curve by adding a path <m>C_1</m> along the x-axis
        that connects <m>(-1,0)</m> and <m>(1,0)</m>.  In that case, along that added path
        <m>C_1</m>, <md>\int_{C_1} \mbf F \cdot \hat \nvec~ds =\int_{C_1}(x,1)\cdot (0,-1)~ds = \int_{C_1}-1~ds = -1 (length \ of \ C_1)=-2. </md>

      </p>
      <p>
        Then <m>\iint_D \nabla\cdot \mbf F ~dA = 1~dA = 2(2)(\frac12)=2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Also, 
        <md>
          \int_{C_1} \mbf F \cdot \hat \nvec ~ds + \int_C \mbf F \hat\nvec~ds=\iint_D \nabla\cdot \mbf F ~dA,
        </md>
        So rearranging, <m>\int_C \mbf F \cdot \hat\nvec~ds = 2-(-2)=4.</m>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="0in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that <m>f(x,y) = x^2+3y+2</m> and <m>C</m> is the spiral parametrized as 
<md>{\bf r}(t) = \left(e^{t/10}\cos t, e^{t/10} \sin t\right)</md>
where <m>0\le t\le 2\pi</m>.  Find <m>\int_C \nabla f\cdot ~dr</m>.
</p>
<image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
      <shortdescription>The spiral curve drawn in the plane, see description.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-9-pic2">
        <diagram dimensions="(150,150)" margins="0">
    <definition>f(t)=(e^(t/10) *cos(t), e^(t/10)*sin(t))</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-3,-3,3,3)">
            <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
            <parametric-curve function="f" domain="(0,2*pi)"
                      N="200" closed="no" arrows="1"/>
            <label anchor="(1,1)" alignment="n" stroke="blue" >
              <m>C</m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
      <description> 
        <p> The spiral parameterized by <m>\mbf r(t)</m> given in the problem.
        </p>
      </description>
    </image>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        There are many ways to do this.  BUT the FTC of line integrals says that 
        <md>
          \int_C \nabla f\cdot d\vec{r} = f(end) - f(start)
        </md>
        (because <m>\mbf F = \nabla f</m> is a conservative field by 
        definition so the path doesn't matter!) and we also know
        that <m>end</m> is when <m>t=2\pi</m>, which is equivalent to the point
        <m>r(2\pi) = (e^{\pi/5}, 0)</m>.  Then
        <m>f(end) = f(e^{\pi}{5},0) = e^{2\pi/5}+2</m>.  Similarly we <m>start</m> at <m>t=0</m> which
        corresponds to <m>r(0)=(1,0)</m>, so <m>f(start)=f(1,0)=1^2+2</m>.  So all together
        <md>
          \int_C \nabla f\cdot d\vec{r} = e^{2\pi/5}+2 - 3 = e^{2\pi/5}-1.
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Work.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the work done by the force <m>{\bf F}(x,y) = (xy,y^2)</m> in moving an object along the straight line segment from <m>(0,0)</m> to <m>(2,4)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        Feel free to draw the curve. It is a line that can be parameterized 
        by <m>y = 2x</m> (and <m>x=x</m>) as <m>x\rightarrow 0 \rightarrow 2</m>. 
        Then <m>dy = 2dx</m>, and we have
        <md>
          <mrow> W = \int_C \mbf F \cdot d\vec r \amp 
           = \int_C xy dx + y^2 dy</mrow>
           <mrow> \amp =\int_0^2 x(2x)dx + (2x)^2(2)dx</mrow>
           <mrow> \amp = \int_0^2 10x^2dx </mrow>
           <mrow> \amp = \frac{80}{3} </mrow>
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the work done by the same force when moving an object from the origin to <m>(2,0)</m> and then <m>(2,4)</m> along straight line segments.</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        Two pieces, draw a picture.  If <m>C_1</m> is the horizontal leg with 
        <m>x=x</m> and <m>y=0</m>, then <m>W = 
          \int_{C_1} xydx+y^2dy = 0
        </m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If <m>C_2</m> is the vertical leg, it happens at 
        <m>x=2</m> and <m>y=y</m> where <m>y</m> goes from 0 to 4. 
        So <md>
          W = \int_{C_2} xydx + y^2 dy = \int_0^4 2y(0)+y^2dy = \frac{64}{3}.
        </md>
        Then total work is <m>0+\frac{64}{3} = \frac{64}{3}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Find the work done by the force <m>{\bf F}(x,y) = (xy,y^2)</m> in moving an object along the rectangle whose vertices are <m>(0,0), (3,0), (3,2), (0,2)</m> in the counter-clockwise direction.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        It's a closed curve, so Green's theorem could probably be lovely here!
        <md>
          \oint_C \mbf F \cdot d\vec r = \oint_C P~dx+Q~dy = \iint_D \left(\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y}\right)~dA
        </md>
        <md>
          = \iint_D \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(y^2) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(xy)\right)~dA
        </md>
        <md>
          =-x~dA = \int_{y=0}^2\int_{x=0}^3 -x ~dxdy = -9.
        </md>
        
        
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that <m>S</m> is the curvy part of the cylinder defined by <m>x^2+y^2 = 4</m> and <m>0\le z \le 2</m> (this is an open cylinder).  Find the flux of the field <m>{\bf F}(x,y,z) = (x,y,z)</m> through <m>S</m> in the outward direction.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        Add the top and bottom disks to close the surface. 
      </p>
      <p>
        The top has <m>\hat \nvec = (0,0,1)</m> so <m>\mbf F\cdot \hat \nvec = z = 2</m> since 
        the top is in the plane <m>z=2</m>.  So 
        <md>
          \iint_{top} \mbf F \cdot \hat \nvec~dA = \iint_{top} 2~dA = 8\pi.
        </md>
        
      </p>
      <p>
        The bottom has <m>\hat \nvec = (0,0,-1)</m> so <m>\mbf F\cdot \hat \nvec = -z = 0</m> since 
        the bottom is in the plane <m>z=0</m>.  So 
        <md>
          \iint_{bottom} \mbf F \cdot \hat \nvec~dA = \iint_{bottom} 0~dA = 0.
        </md>
        
      </p>
      <p>
        Then the divergence part:
        <md>
          \iiint_E \nabla \cdot \mbf F ~dV = \iiint_E 3~dV = 24\pi.
        </md>
        
      </p>
      <p>
        All together now...
        <md>
          \iint_S \mbf F \cdot \hat \nvec ~dA = \iiint_E \nabla\cdot \mbf F dV - \iint_{top}\mbf F \cdot \hat \nvec ~dA -\iint_{bottom}\mbf F \cdot \hat \nvec ~dA =24\pi-8\pi-0=16\pi.
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="0.4in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that <m>{\bf F}(x,y)</m> is a field with <m>\nabla \times {\bf F} = 0</m> except at the two indicated points below with x's.  Suppose also that 
<md>\oint_{C_1} {\bf F}\cdot ~d{\bf r} = 3, \quad \quad \oint_{C_2} {\bf F}\cdot ~d{\bf r} = -7</md>
What is <m>\oint_{C} {\bf F}\cdot ~d{\bf r}</m>?
</p>
<image width="25%" margins="0% 75%">
      <shortdescription>See description below.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-9-pic3">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="5">
    
          <coordinates bbox="(-7,-5,7.3,4)">
            <axes  decorations="no" axes="none"/>
            <spline points="((7,0),(5,2),(3,1.3),(0,2),(-4,0.3),(-6,-2),(-4,-4),(0,-2),(4,-2),(7,0))"
            closed="yes" stroke="blue" arrows="1" N="1000" arrow-location="3.2"/>
            <label anchor="(0,2)" alignment="ne" offset="(-4,3)">
              <m>C</m>
            </label>

            <spline points="((4,0),(3,1),(2,0),(3,-1),(4,0))"
            closed="yes" stroke="blue" arrows="1" N="1000" arrow-location="0.2"/>
            <label anchor="(4,0)" alignment="e" offset="(-4,-7)">
              <m>C_1</m>
            </label>
            <point p="(3,0)" style="cross" thickness="3" stroke="red"/>
            <spline points="((-1,-1),(-2,0),(-3.5,-1),(-2,-2),(-1,-1))"
            closed="yes" stroke="blue" arrows="1" N="1000" arrow-location="0.2"/>
            <label anchor="(-2,0)" alignment="ne" offset="(0,-7)">
              <m>C_2</m>
            </label>
            <point p="(-2,-1)" style="cross" thickness="3" stroke="red"/>
    
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
      <description>
        <p>
        One squiggly closed curve <m>C</m> traveling counterclockwise and 
        encompassing two other closed curves <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_1</m>
        which also travel counterclockwise and do not intersect.  Both <m>C_1</m>
        and <m>C_2</m> encompass a spot where <m>\nabla \times \mbf F</m> is not zero, 
        marked on the graph with an "x".
        </p>
      </description>
    </image>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        If we connect <m>C_1</m> and <m>C_2</m> via a path there and back with an
        infinitely small gap between them, then connect <m>C_1</m> and <m>C</m> in the same way, 
        then the inside of that resulting fun shape is simply connected and within on closed curve, 
        <md>
           \oint_{C-C_1-C_2} \mbf F \cdot d\vec r = \iint_D \nabla \times \mbf F ~dA = 0.
        </md>
        This implies that <m>\oint_C \mbf F \cdot d\vec r = \int_{C_1}  \mbf F \cdot d\vec r +\int_{C_2}  \mbf F \cdot d\vec r =3+(-7)=-4.</m>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let <m>T</m> be a hollow conducting sphere with radius <m>R</m>, with charge density of <m>\sigma</m> distributed equally across the surface of the sphere where <m>\sigma</m> is positive.  (Just a reminder that the sphere is hollow and all charge lives on the surface of the sphere!)</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the electric field both within the hollow sphere, and outside of the hollow sphere.</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        Gauss! Break up into two pieces.
      </p>
      <p>
        Inside the hollow sphere: the Gaussian surface is just a smaller sphere that shares the same 
        center point but nests completely inside.  But, the Gaussian surface has 
        no charge within it, since all the charge is on the surface of the hollow sphere which is outside 
        of the Gaussian surface.  So no charge enclosed means Gauss' Law says 
        <m>\vec {\mbf E} = \vec 0</m> within the sphere.
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside the hollow sphere.  The Gaussian surface is still a sphere with a bigger radius, give it radius
        <m>r\gt R</m>.  So we will want to use Gauss' Law:
        <md>
          \oiint_S \vec{\mbf E}\cdot \hat \nvec~dA = \frac{(total \ charge \ within \ S)}{\epsilon_0}
        </md>
        The left: symmetry (yay) says the electric field is constant on the surface of the Gaussian 
        surface <m>S</m>, and the area of <m>S</m> is the surface area of a sphere with 
        radius <m>r</m>, which is <m>4\pi r^2</m>.  Also the 
        electric field and normal vector point the same way.  So 
        <md>
          \oiint_S \vec{\mbf E}\cdot \hat \nvec~dA = \oiint_S |\vec{\mbf E}|~dA = |\vec{\mbf E}|(area \ of \ S) = |\vec{\mbf E}|(4\pi r^2).
        </md>
        The right: Charge inside the Gaussian surface is just all the 
        charge on the original hollow sphere, which is the charge times the surface area of the hollow sphere (not the Gaussian surface!), 
        or <m>\sigma (4\pi R^2)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        All together.... (equate the two using Gauss:)
        <md>
          |\vec{\mbf E}|(4\pi r^2) = \frac{\sigma (4\pi R^2)}{\epsilon_0}
        </md>
        and rearrange:
        <md>
          |\vec{\mbf E}| = \frac{\sigma R^2}{\epsilon_0 r^2}.
        </md>
        We also know that <m>\vec{\mbf E}</m> will be in the outward normal direction, so
        <m>{\vec{\mbf E} = \frac{\sigma R^2}{\epsilon_0 r^2} \hat \nvec}</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        Thus, including inside and outside, <m>\vec{\mbf E} = \begin{cases} 0, \amp r \lt R \\ 
           \frac{\sigma R^2}{\epsilon_0 r^2} \hat \nvec , \amp r \ge R \end{cases} </m>
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find the electric potential at a distance <m>r</m> from the center of the sphere.</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S1-8-soln">
      <p>
        Note, <m>\vec{\mbf E}</m> and <m>d\vec r</m> point the same way, and 
        so <m>\vec{\mbf E} \cdot d\vec r = |\vec{\mbf E}| dr</m>. We'll actually still have two pieces, inside and outside the hollow sphere.
        <md>
          f(r) = \int_C \vec{\mbf E} \cdot d\vec r = \int_{0}^R |\vec{\mbf E}|dr + \int_{R}^{r} |\vec{\mbf E}|dr
        </md>
        <md>
          = 0 + \int_R^r  \frac{\sigma R^2}{\epsilon_0 r^2} ~dr =  \frac{\sigma R^2}{\epsilon_0}\int_R^r \frac1{r^2}~dr
        </md>
        <md>
          = \frac{\sigma R^2}{\epsilon_0 }\left.\left(-\frac1r\right)\right|_R^r 

        </md>
        <md>
          =  \frac{\sigma R^2}{\epsilon_0}\left(-\frac1r + \frac1R\right).
        </md>
        Note that if <m>r\lt R</m>, you're inside the sphere, and you never get to the 
        non-zero electric field.  That is you never get to the second integral from <m>R</m> 
        to <m>r</m>, so putting it together we have 
        <md>
          f(r) = \begin{cases} 0 , \amp r \lt R \\ 
            \frac{\sigma R^2}{\epsilon_0}\left(-\frac1r + \frac1R\right), \amp r \ge R\end{cases}
        </md>
        Though it may be piecewise, it is continuous...
        
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
